// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 6): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"dishwashers",t:"Dishwashers",c:"Technology",i:"🍽️",w:"Dishwasher",r:["washingmachine","vacuumcleaner","feminism","elec","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Machines for dishes","Dishwashers clean plates, cups and cutlery with hot water sprays."],["Invented by a socialite","Josephine Cochrane patented a practical dishwasher in 1886."],["Saving water","Modern dishwashers often use less water than washing by hand."]],
l:"A dishwasher is a machine that cleans dishes, glasses, cutlery and cookware automatically. Dishes are loaded into racks, and the machine sprays them with hot water and detergent from rotating arms, then rinses and dries them. Early hand-cranked designs appeared in the 1850s, but the first practical dishwasher was invented by Josephine Cochrane, a wealthy woman from Illinois who wanted a machine that would wash dishes faster than her servants and without chipping her fine china. She built a machine in which dishes sat in wire compartments inside a wheel while water was sprayed onto them, patented it in 1886 and showed it at the 1893 World’s Columbian Exposition in Chicago, where it won an award. Her company later became part of KitchenAid. Dishwashers were used mainly in hotels and restaurants until after World War II, when smaller home models spread. Studies have found that modern efficient dishwashers usually use less water and energy than washing the same dishes by hand under running water.",
s:[["How it works","The machine heats water, often to about 50–70°C, and pumps it through spinning spray arms. Detergents contain enzymes that break down proteins and starches. A filter catches food scraps, and the dirty water is pumped out. Some models dry dishes with heat; others use the residual heat and condensation."],["Josephine Cochrane","Cochrane, a descendant of the inventor of an early steamboat, worked in a shed behind her house with a mechanic to build her machine. After her husband died, leaving debts, she turned the invention into a business, selling it first to hotels and restaurants."],["Water and energy","A modern dishwasher may use about 10–15 liters of water per cycle, while washing a full load by hand under a running tap can use several times as much. Eco programs use lower temperatures for longer times. Scraping dishes instead of pre-rinsing them saves more water."],["Around the world","Dishwashers are common in North America and Western Europe but much less so in many other countries, where kitchens are smaller and hand-washing is traditional. In Japan and China, compact countertop dishwashers have become more popular."]],
k:[["Practical inventor","Josephine Cochrane"],["Patent","1886"],["Award","1893 Chicago World’s Fair"],["Water per cycle","About 10–15 liters (modern)"],["Main parts","Racks, spray arms, pump, filter"]],
d:[["1850","Joel Houghton patents a hand-turned dish-washing device"],["1886","Josephine Cochrane patents her dishwasher"],["1893","Cochrane’s machine wins an award in Chicago"],["1949","KitchenAid sells a home dishwasher"],["1970s","Dishwashers become common in American homes"]],
s2:[["What not to put in","Wooden utensils, cast-iron pans, sharp knives and some plastics can be damaged by the heat and detergents. Hand-painted china and crystal may fade or cloud over many washes."],["Inventors’ Hall of Fame","Josephine Cochrane was inducted into the US National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2006, recognizing one of the most important household inventions by a woman."]],
sp:["Cooking in the dishwasher","Some cooks have experimented with ‘dishwasher cooking’, sealing salmon or vegetables in jars or bags and cooking them gently in the hot water of a wash cycle, a novelty that works like low-temperature sous-vide cooking."],
g:[["Detergent","A cleaning chemical"],["Enzyme","A protein that speeds up chemical reactions"],["Cycle","One complete run of a machine"],["Condensation","Water vapor turning into liquid"]],
f:["Josephine Cochrane invented the practical dishwasher.","Modern dishwashers often save water.","Cochrane’s company became part of KitchenAid."],
fun:"Josephine Cochrane reportedly said, ‘If nobody else is going to invent a dishwashing machine, I’ll do it myself.’",
tl:"Naglilinis ang dishwasher ng mga plato, tasa at kubyertos gamit ang wisik ng mainit na tubig. Inimbento ni Josephine Cochrane ang praktikal na dishwasher noong 1886, at kadalasang mas kaunting tubig ang gamit nito kaysa sa paghuhugas sa kamay."});

ADDART({id:"microphones",t:"Microphones",c:"Technology",i:"🎤",w:"Microphone",r:["sound","headphones","radio","phone","smartphone"],
p:[["Capturing sound","Microphones turn sound waves into electrical signals."],["From telephones to studios","Microphones developed with the telephone and radio."],["Tiny chips","Smartphones use microphones on microchips."]],
l:"A microphone is a device that converts sound waves into electrical signals that can be amplified, recorded or transmitted. Sound waves make a thin diaphragm vibrate, and the microphone turns those vibrations into a changing electric current. Microphones were developed along with the telephone: Alexander Graham Bell used a liquid transmitter in 1876, and in 1877–1878 Emile Berliner, Thomas Edison and David Edward Hughes developed carbon microphones, in which sound pressed carbon granules together, changing their resistance; carbon microphones were used in telephones for about a century. The condenser microphone, invented by E. C. Wente at Bell Labs in 1916, and the dynamic and ribbon microphones of the 1920s and 1930s made high-quality radio broadcasting and recording possible. Today billions of tiny MEMS microphones, built on silicon chips, are used in phones, laptops, hearing aids and smart speakers. Different microphones pick up sound from different directions, and singers, broadcasters and filmmakers choose them for particular uses.",
s:[["Types","Dynamic microphones use a coil attached to a diaphragm moving in a magnetic field, like a loudspeaker in reverse; they are tough and common on stage. Condenser microphones use a diaphragm forming one plate of a capacitor and need power; they are sensitive and used in studios. Ribbon microphones use a thin metal ribbon and give a warm sound."],["Directional patterns","Omnidirectional microphones pick up sound from all directions. Cardioid microphones, named after their heart-shaped pickup pattern, mainly capture sound from the front, reducing noise from the audience. Shotgun microphones, used in film, pick up sound from a narrow direction at a distance."],["Microphones and music","Microphones changed singing. Before them, singers had to project loudly to fill halls. From the late 1920s, ‘crooners’ such as Bing Crosby sang softly and intimately into microphones, and the electric microphone made modern popular music possible."],["MEMS microphones","Micro-electromechanical systems (MEMS) microphones are a few millimeters across and are made like computer chips. A modern smartphone may contain three or more, used together to cancel background noise and detect the direction of a voice."]],
k:[["Converts","Sound to electrical signals"],["Carbon microphone","1877–1878"],["Condenser microphone","E. C. Wente, 1916"],["Stage favorite","Dynamic microphone"],["In phones","MEMS microphones"]],
d:[["1876","Bell’s liquid transmitter carries speech"],["1878","Carbon microphones developed"],["1916","Condenser microphone invented"],["1931","Ribbon microphone developed by RCA"],["2000s","MEMS microphones spread into phones"]],
s2:[["The word","The word ‘microphone’ was used in the 1820s for an acoustic device to amplify faint sounds. David Edward Hughes applied it to his electrical carbon device in 1878, showing that it could make the footsteps of a fly audible."],["Mic drop","Dropping a microphone at the end of a performance, the ‘mic drop’, became a popular gesture of triumph. Sound engineers dislike it because it can damage microphones and speakers."]],
sp:["Feedback","The loud squeal heard at concerts and speeches is acoustic feedback, caused when a microphone picks up sound from a nearby loudspeaker, which is amplified again in a loop. Positioning microphones behind speakers prevents it."],
g:[["Diaphragm","A thin sheet that vibrates with sound"],["Capacitor","A device that stores electric charge"],["Cardioid","A heart-shaped pickup pattern"],["MEMS","Tiny mechanical devices built on chips"]],
f:["Carbon microphones were used in telephones for about a century.","Phones use tiny microphones built like chips.","A dynamic microphone is like a loudspeaker in reverse."],
fun:"David Edward Hughes demonstrated that his early microphone could make the sound of a fly walking on a box audible.",
tl:"Ginagawang senyas na elektrikal ng mikropono ang mga alon ng tunog. Nabuo ito kasabay ng telepono at radyo, at gumagamit ang mga smartphone ng napakaliit na mikropono sa microchip."});

ADDART({id:"smokedetectors",t:"Smoke Detectors",c:"Technology",i:"🚨",w:"Smoke detector",r:["firefighters","radioact","elec","smarthome","sound"],
p:[["Early warning","Smoke detectors sound an alarm when they sense smoke."],["Saving lives","They greatly reduce deaths in house fires."],["Two main types","Ionization and photoelectric detectors sense smoke differently."]],
l:"A smoke detector, or smoke alarm, is a device that senses smoke and sounds a loud alarm to warn people of a fire. Most home fire deaths happen at night, when people are asleep and may not smell smoke, so smoke alarms give crucial extra minutes to escape. Early smoke detectors were developed in the 1930s by Swiss physicist Walter Jaeger, who discovered by accident that cigarette smoke affected an ionization sensor he was building to detect poison gas. Affordable battery-powered home smoke alarms appeared in the United States in the late 1960s and 1970s, and many countries now require them by law in homes. There are two main types: ionization alarms, which use a tiny amount of radioactive americium-241 to detect fast-flaming fires, and photoelectric alarms, which use a light beam and react faster to slow, smoldering fires. Studies by the US National Fire Protection Association show that the risk of dying in a home fire is about 60 percent lower in homes with working smoke alarms. Alarms must be tested regularly and replaced about every ten years.",
s:[["Ionization alarms","Inside an ionization chamber, a tiny source of americium-241 ionizes the air, letting a small current flow between two plates. Smoke particles disrupt the current, triggering the alarm. The amount of radioactive material is very small and safe in normal use."],["Photoelectric alarms","In a photoelectric alarm, a light source shines away from a sensor. When smoke enters, particles scatter light onto the sensor, setting off the alarm. Photoelectric alarms are less prone to false alarms from cooking and respond faster to smoldering fires such as those started by cigarettes."],["Keeping alarms working","Many fire deaths occur in homes where alarms have dead or removed batteries, often taken out because of nuisance alarms. Sealed alarms with ten-year batteries, interconnected alarms that all sound together and alarms with strobe lights or bed shakers for deaf people improve safety."],["Carbon monoxide alarms","Carbon monoxide, produced by faulty heaters and boilers, is a colorless, odorless poison gas. Separate or combined carbon monoxide alarms detect it and are required in many homes with fuel-burning appliances."]],
k:[["Main types","Ionization and photoelectric"],["Ionization source","Americium-241"],["Early inventor","Walter Jaeger (1930s)"],["Death risk with working alarms","About 60% lower (NFPA)"],["Replace alarms","About every 10 years"]],
d:[["1890","Early electric fire alarm patented in the US"],["1930s","Walter Jaeger discovers smoke sets off an ionization sensor"],["1965","Battery-powered home smoke alarm patented"],["1970s","Home smoke alarms become widespread in the US"],["1990s","Many countries require smoke alarms in new homes"]],
s2:[["The accidental discovery","Walter Jaeger was trying to build a sensor for poison gas. Frustrated when it did not work, he lit a cigarette, and the sensor responded to the smoke, leading to the first ionization smoke detectors used in industry."],["Test once a month","Fire services recommend pressing the test button monthly and having an alarm on every level of a home, outside sleeping areas. Many recommend changing clocks and checking alarms at the same time."]],
sp:["The chirp","A smoke alarm that chirps every minute or so is usually warning that its battery is low. Many chirps happen at night because cooling temperatures reduce battery voltage."],
g:[["Ionization","Giving atoms an electric charge"],["Photoelectric","Using light to detect something"],["Americium-241","A radioactive element used in smoke alarms"],["Carbon monoxide","A poisonous, odorless gas"]],
f:["Working smoke alarms cut the risk of dying in a fire by about 60%.","Ionization alarms contain a tiny radioactive source.","Smoke alarms should be replaced about every ten years."],
fun:"The first smoke detector was discovered by accident when an inventor lit a cigarette near a gas sensor that was not working.",
tl:"Tumutunog ang smoke detector kapag nakadarama ito ng usok. Malaki ang ibinababa nito sa bilang ng namamatay sa sunog sa bahay, at may dalawang pangunahing uri: ionization at photoelectric."});

ADDART({id:"atms",t:"ATMs",c:"Technology",i:"🏧",w:"Automated teller machine",r:["money","digitalpay","cybersecurity","passwords","cryptocurrency"],
p:[["Cash machines","ATMs let people withdraw cash and check accounts without a bank teller."],["First in London","The first cash machine opened in London in 1967."],["Fewer ATMs","As digital payments grow, the number of ATMs is falling in many countries."]],
l:"An automated teller machine (ATM), also called a cash machine or cashpoint, is an electronic device that lets bank customers withdraw cash, check balances and sometimes deposit money or pay bills without visiting a bank teller. Users insert a bank card or tap a phone and enter a personal identification number (PIN). The first cash machine was installed by Barclays bank in Enfield, north London, on 27 June 1967, designed by a team led by John Shepherd-Barron; it used paper vouchers treated with radioactive carbon-14 rather than plastic cards. Other machines appeared in Sweden, Japan and the United States around the same time, and ATMs linked to bank computers spread worldwide in the 1970s and 1980s, letting people get cash at any hour. There are roughly 3 million ATMs worldwide. As card payments, mobile wallets and instant transfers grow, ATM numbers are falling in many countries, raising concerns that people who rely on cash, often older, poorer or rural people, could be left behind. Criminals target ATMs with card skimmers, malware and physical attacks.",
s:[["The first cash machine","Shepherd-Barron said he got the idea in his bath after arriving at his bank too late to withdraw money. The first machine’s first customer was the comedy actor Reg Varney. He chose a four-digit PIN because his wife could remember only four numbers."],["How ATMs work","ATMs connect to bank networks that check whether the card is valid and whether the account has enough money. Machines contain locked cassettes of banknotes and count notes using sensors. Networks such as Visa and Mastercard let people use ATMs of other banks and in other countries."],["Security","Skimmers are hidden devices attached to card slots to copy card details, while tiny cameras record PINs. Banks have added anti-skimming hardware and chip cards. Criminals have also used ‘jackpotting’ malware to make machines spit out cash, and explosive attacks led banks to install ink-staining systems."],["A cashless future?","In Sweden, cash use has fallen sharply, and many bank branches no longer handle cash. The United Kingdom and other countries have introduced rules to protect access to cash, requiring banks to provide cash services in communities."]],
k:[["First cash machine","Barclays, Enfield, London, 27 June 1967"],["Inventor credited","John Shepherd-Barron"],["Security code","PIN"],["Number worldwide","About 3 million"],["Trend","Falling in many countries"]],
d:[["1967","First cash machine opens in London"],["1969","First ATM in the United States, in New York"],["1970s","ATMs linked to bank networks"],["1990s","Chip-and-PIN cards developed"],["2010s","ATM numbers begin to fall in many countries"]],
s2:[["A gold ATM","Some ATMs dispense gold bars and coins, such as machines introduced in Dubai and Germany in 2010. Bitcoin ATMs, which exchange cash for cryptocurrency, have also spread, though authorities warn they are often used in scams."],["The Vatican’s Latin ATM","An ATM in Vatican City offers instructions in Latin, among other languages, one of the few machines in the world to do so."]],
sp:["Radioactive vouchers","Customers using the first London cash machine inserted a paper voucher marked with carbon-14. The machine detected the radioactivity, matched it with a PIN and paid out a fixed amount of 10 pounds."],
g:[["PIN","Personal identification number"],["Skimmer","A device that secretly copies card data"],["Teller","A bank employee who handles cash"],["Cashless","Using no physical money"]],
f:["The first cash machine opened in London in 1967.","The first PIN was four digits because of a wife’s memory.","ATM numbers are falling in many countries."],
fun:"The first person to use a cash machine was a television comedy actor, Reg Varney, in 1967.",
tl:"Pinapayagan ng ATM ang mga tao na kumuha ng pera at tingnan ang account nang walang teller ng bangko. Binuksan ang unang cash machine sa London noong 1967, at bumababa na ang bilang ng ATM sa maraming bansa."});

ADDART({id:"heatpumps",t:"Heat Pumps",c:"Technology",i:"♨️",w:"Heat pump",r:["refrigeration","thermo","energytransition","elec","climchange"],
p:[["Moving heat","Heat pumps move heat from outside air, ground or water into buildings."],["Very efficient","They can deliver three or more units of heat for each unit of electricity."],["Cutting emissions","Heat pumps are key to replacing gas and oil boilers."]],
l:"A heat pump is a machine that moves heat from one place to another using electricity, rather than producing heat by burning fuel. It works like a refrigerator in reverse: a refrigerant fluid absorbs heat from the outside air, the ground or water, even when they are cold, is compressed to raise its temperature, and releases the heat indoors; many heat pumps can also run backward to cool buildings in summer. Because they move heat rather than create it, heat pumps are very efficient, typically delivering three to four units of heat for every unit of electricity they use. Heat pumps are central to plans to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from heating, which accounts for a large share of energy use in cold countries, especially when powered by low-carbon electricity. Norway, Sweden and Finland use heat pumps in a large share of homes, and they are common in Japan and China. According to the International Energy Agency, global sales fell slightly in 2025, but European sales rose 11 percent after two years of decline, led by Germany.",
s:[["How it works","A heat pump has four main parts: an evaporator, where cold refrigerant absorbs heat; a compressor, which squeezes it and makes it hot; a condenser, where it releases heat into the home; and an expansion valve, which lowers its pressure so the cycle can repeat. Air-source heat pumps take heat from outside air; ground-source pumps use pipes buried in the ground."],["Efficiency","Efficiency is measured by the coefficient of performance (COP): a COP of 3 means 3 kilowatt-hours of heat for each kilowatt-hour of electricity. Efficiency falls in very cold weather, but modern air-source heat pumps work at temperatures below −20°C, as shown by their popularity in Norway."],["History","The principle was described by Lord Kelvin in 1852. Austrian engineer Peter von Rittinger built an early heat pump in 1856 to evaporate salt water. The first large heat pump to heat a building was installed in Zurich’s city hall in 1938, using water from the Limmat River."],["Challenges","Heat pumps cost more to buy and install than gas boilers, and they work best in well-insulated homes. Governments offer grants to encourage them. Some older refrigerants are powerful greenhouse gases, so manufacturers are switching to alternatives such as propane and carbon dioxide."]],
k:[["Works by","Moving heat, not burning fuel"],["Typical efficiency","3–4 units of heat per unit of electricity"],["Main types","Air-source and ground-source"],["First building heat pump","Zurich City Hall, 1938"],["2025 sales","Global −2%; Europe +11% (IEA)"]],
d:[["1852","Lord Kelvin describes the heat pump principle"],["1856","Peter von Rittinger builds an early heat pump"],["1938","Heat pump installed in Zurich City Hall"],["1970s","Oil crises boost interest in heat pumps"],["2022","Sales surge in Europe after gas prices spike"],["2025","European heat pump sales rise again"]],
s2:[["Norway","Norway has one of the highest numbers of heat pumps per household in the world, with most homes using one. Cheap hydroelectric power and cold winters make heat pumps an obvious choice there."],["Heat pumps in your kitchen","A refrigerator is a heat pump: it pumps heat out of the food compartment and releases it from coils at the back, which is why the back of a fridge feels warm."]],
sp:["Heat from rivers and sewers","Large heat pumps can warm whole districts using heat from rivers, the sea, sewage or data centers. Helsinki and Stockholm use heat pumps to recover heat from treated wastewater."],
g:[["Refrigerant","A fluid that absorbs and releases heat"],["Compressor","A device that squeezes gas"],["Coefficient of performance","A measure of heat pump efficiency"],["Ground-source","Taking heat from the ground"]],
f:["Heat pumps can deliver three to four times more heat than the electricity they use.","A refrigerator is a kind of heat pump.","European heat pump sales rose 11% in 2025."],
fun:"Even air at −10°C contains plenty of heat energy, which a heat pump can extract and concentrate to warm a house.",
tl:"Inililipat ng heat pump ang init mula sa hangin, lupa o tubig sa labas papasok sa mga gusali. Napakahusay nito, nakapagbibigay ng tatlo o higit pang yunit ng init sa bawat yunit ng kuryente, at mahalaga ito sa pagpapalit sa mga boiler na gumagamit ng gas at langis."});

ADDART({id:"lidar",t:"Lidar",c:"Technology",i:"📡",w:"Lidar",r:["laser","radar","selfdriving","maya","smartphone"],
p:[["Measuring with lasers","Lidar measures distances by timing reflected laser pulses."],["Seeing through forests","Airborne lidar reveals ruins hidden under jungle."],["Eyes of robots","Lidar helps self-driving cars and robots see in 3D."]],
l:"Lidar, short for ‘light detection and ranging’, is a technology that measures distances by firing pulses of laser light and timing how long they take to bounce back from objects. Because light travels at a known speed, each returning pulse gives a precise distance, and a scanner firing hundreds of thousands of pulses per second can build a detailed three-dimensional ‘point cloud’ of its surroundings. Lidar was developed in the 1960s, soon after the invention of the laser; in 1971, Apollo 15 astronauts used a laser altimeter to map the Moon’s surface. Lidar mounted on aircraft and drones maps landscapes, forests, floodplains and coastlines, and because some pulses pass through gaps in leaves, it can reveal the ground beneath dense forest. This has led to spectacular archaeological discoveries, including thousands of hidden Maya structures in Central America. Lidar is used in self-driving cars, robots, smartphones and tablets for depth sensing, and by scientists to study clouds, air pollution and ice sheets.",
s:[["How it works","A lidar sensor sends a laser pulse and measures its return time, often within a billionth of a second. Combined with GPS and motion sensors that record the device’s exact position and orientation, the measurements form a precise 3D map. Some pulses return from the treetops, others from branches, and some from the ground."],["Lost cities","In 2018, archaeologists announced that airborne lidar over northern Guatemala had revealed more than 60,000 previously unknown Maya structures, including houses, causeways and fortifications. Later surveys in Mexico, the Amazon and Cambodia, around Angkor, uncovered whole hidden cities."],["Self-driving cars","Many autonomous vehicles use spinning or solid-state lidar units on their roofs to detect cars, pedestrians and obstacles in 3D, day or night. Some companies, such as Tesla, rely on cameras instead, arguing lidar is unnecessary; the debate continues."],["Science from space","NASA’s ICESat-2 satellite, launched in 2018, uses lidar to measure the height of ice sheets and sea ice to within a few centimeters, tracking how polar ice is melting. Space lidar also measures forest heights to estimate how much carbon they store."]],
k:[["Stands for","Light detection and ranging"],["Uses","Laser pulses"],["Output","3D point clouds"],["Famous discovery","60,000+ hidden Maya structures (2018)"],["Space example","ICESat-2 (2018)"]],
d:[["1960","First laser built"],["1971","Apollo 15 maps the Moon with a laser altimeter"],["2005","Lidar-equipped robot cars compete in the DARPA Grand Challenge"],["2018","Lidar reveals 60,000 Maya structures in Guatemala"],["2018","ICESat-2 launched"],["2020","Lidar sensors added to smartphones and tablets"]],
s2:[["Lidar in your pocket","Some smartphones and tablets released from 2020 include small lidar scanners that help cameras focus in the dark, measure rooms and place augmented reality objects accurately."],["Radar, sonar and lidar","Radar uses radio waves, sonar uses sound and lidar uses light. Lidar gives much finer detail than radar but is affected more by fog, rain and dust."]],
sp:["Angkor’s hidden cities","Lidar surveys in Cambodia in 2012 and 2015 revealed that the area around Angkor Wat once contained a vast urban landscape of roads, canals and neighborhoods hidden under forest, much larger than previously known."],
g:[["Laser","A device producing a narrow, intense beam of light"],["Point cloud","A set of 3D points representing a surface"],["Altimeter","A device that measures height"],["Autonomous vehicle","A self-driving vehicle"]],
f:["Lidar measures distances with laser pulses.","Lidar revealed tens of thousands of hidden Maya structures.","Some smartphones contain lidar scanners."],
fun:"Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon, and scientists still bounce lasers off them to measure the Moon’s distance to within millimeters.",
tl:"Sinusukat ng lidar ang layo sa pamamagitan ng pagbilang ng oras ng bumabalik na mga pulso ng laser. Ibinubunyag ng lidar mula sa eroplano ang mga guho sa ilalim ng gubat, at tumutulong ito sa mga self-driving car at robot na makakita nang 3D."});

ADDART({id:"pacemakers",t:"Pacemakers",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💓",w:"Artificial cardiac pacemaker",r:["heart","heartdisease","surgery","battery","prosthetics"],
p:[["Keeping the beat","Pacemakers send electrical pulses to keep the heart beating regularly."],["First implant in 1958","The first implantable pacemaker was fitted in Sweden in 1958."],["Smaller every year","Modern leadless pacemakers are smaller than a coin."]],
l:"An artificial pacemaker is a small medical device that uses electrical pulses to make the heart beat at a normal rate when its natural pacemaker, a cluster of cells called the sinoatrial node, or its electrical pathways do not work properly. Conditions such as an abnormally slow heartbeat or heart block can cause tiredness, dizziness and fainting. A typical pacemaker has a battery-powered generator placed under the skin below the collarbone and one or more thin wires, called leads, threaded through veins into the heart. It monitors the heart’s rhythm and sends a pulse only when needed. The first external pacemakers of the 1950s were bulky machines plugged into the wall. In 1958, Swedish engineer Rune Elmqvist and surgeon Åke Senning implanted the first fully internal pacemaker in Arne Larsson, who went on to receive 26 devices and lived until 2001. Today more than a million pacemakers are implanted each year worldwide. Batteries last about 7 to 15 years, and leadless pacemakers implanted directly into the heart were approved in 2016.",
s:[["How it works","Sensors in the pacemaker detect the heart’s electrical activity. If the heart beats too slowly or pauses, the pacemaker sends a tiny pulse that makes the heart muscle contract. Many modern pacemakers can increase the heart rate during exercise by sensing movement or breathing."],["Arne Larsson","Arne Larsson, a 43-year-old Swedish engineer, had a heart condition that caused him to faint many times a day. His wife persuaded doctors to try an experimental implanted pacemaker. The first device lasted only hours, but he lived another 43 years with later pacemakers, outliving both his surgeon and the engineer."],["Leadless pacemakers","Leadless pacemakers, about the size of a large vitamin capsule, are inserted through a vein in the leg and attached directly inside the heart. They avoid problems with leads, such as breakage and infection. Wireless monitoring lets doctors check pacemakers remotely."],["Defibrillators","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs) are related devices that detect dangerous fast heart rhythms and deliver an electric shock to restore a normal beat. Many devices combine pacemaker and defibrillator functions."]],
k:[["Purpose","Keeps the heart beating at a normal rate"],["First implant","Sweden, 1958"],["First patient","Arne Larsson"],["Battery life","About 7–15 years"],["Leadless pacemakers","Approved 2016"]],
d:[["1932","Albert Hyman builds an early external pacemaker"],["1950","External pacemaker developed in Canada"],["1958","First implantable pacemaker"],["1960","First long-lasting implantable pacemaker in the US"],["2016","First leadless pacemaker approved in the US"]],
s2:[["Pacemakers and phones","Strong magnets and some electronic devices can interfere with pacemakers. Patients are advised to keep phones and magnetic earbuds at least about 15 centimeters from the device. Modern pacemakers are well shielded from most everyday electronics."],["Nuclear pacemakers","In the 1970s, some pacemakers were powered by small amounts of plutonium-238, giving batteries that lasted decades. A few patients still had working nuclear pacemakers in the 2000s, but lithium batteries replaced them."]],
sp:["Wilson Greatbatch","American engineer Wilson Greatbatch invented a practical implantable pacemaker in 1958 after accidentally fitting the wrong resistor into a heart-recording device, which produced regular pulses. He later developed long-lasting lithium batteries for pacemakers."],
g:[["Sinoatrial node","The heart’s natural pacemaker"],["Lead","A wire carrying pulses to the heart"],["Heart block","A blockage of the heart’s electrical signals"],["Defibrillator","A device that shocks the heart into a normal rhythm"]],
f:["The first implanted pacemaker was fitted in 1958.","Arne Larsson received 26 pacemakers.","Leadless pacemakers are smaller than a coin."],
fun:"Arne Larsson, the first person to receive an implanted pacemaker, lived to the age of 86.",
tl:"Nagpapadala ang pacemaker ng mga pulsong elektrikal upang mapanatiling regular ang tibok ng puso. Inilagay ang unang implantable pacemaker sa Sweden noong 1958, at mas maliit pa sa barya ang mga makabagong leadless pacemaker."});

ADDART({id:"fermiparadox",t:"The Fermi Paradox",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👽",w:"Fermi paradox",r:["astrobio","exoplanet","milky","ufos","radioastronomy"],
p:[["Where is everybody?","The Fermi paradox asks why we see no sign of alien civilizations."],["A big galaxy","The Milky Way has billions of stars and planets, many possibly habitable."],["Many possible answers","Explanations range from rare life to aliens choosing to hide."]],
l:"The Fermi paradox is the apparent contradiction between the high probability that intelligent alien civilizations exist and the lack of any evidence for them. It is named after Italian-American physicist Enrico Fermi, who, during a lunch conversation at Los Alamos in 1950, reportedly asked, ‘But where is everybody?’ The argument runs that the Milky Way contains hundreds of billions of stars, many with planets in habitable zones, and is about 13 billion years old; even a civilization traveling far slower than light could have spread across the galaxy in a few million years. Yet we have found no alien signals, probes or visitors. In 1961, astronomer Frank Drake devised the Drake equation to estimate the number of communicating civilizations, though most of its factors are unknown. Proposed solutions include that life or intelligence is extremely rare (the ‘Rare Earth’ hypothesis), that civilizations destroy themselves or face a ‘Great Filter’, that interstellar travel is impractical, that aliens are deliberately avoiding us, or that we have not searched long or carefully enough. The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) continues to look for signals.",
s:[["The Drake equation","The Drake equation multiplies the rate of star formation, the fraction of stars with planets, the number of habitable planets per system, and the fractions where life, intelligence and communication arise, by how long civilizations last. Astronomers now know many of the astronomical factors, but the biological ones remain guesses ranging from almost zero to very high."],["The Great Filter","Economist Robin Hanson proposed in 1996 that some step from dead planet to galaxy-spanning civilization must be extremely hard: a ‘Great Filter’. If it lies in our past, such as the origin of life, we may be rare; if it lies ahead, it might threaten our future."],["Other explanations","Some suggest that civilizations communicate in ways we do not recognize, that they are too far apart in space and time, or that the galaxy is quiet because advanced life avoids broadcasting. The ‘zoo hypothesis’ imagines aliens watching but not interfering. Some argue that intelligence like ours may simply be unusual."],["Searching","SETI projects have listened for radio signals since Frank Drake’s Project Ozma in 1960. The 1977 ‘Wow! signal’ remains unexplained but was never detected again. The Breakthrough Listen project, launched in 2015, is the largest search so far. Astronomers also look for ‘technosignatures’ such as unusual light or heat from distant stars."]],
k:[["Named after","Enrico Fermi (1950)"],["Key question","‘Where is everybody?’"],["Drake equation","1961"],["Famous unexplained signal","Wow! signal (1977)"],["Largest search","Breakthrough Listen (since 2015)"]],
d:[["1950","Fermi asks ‘Where is everybody?’"],["1960","Project Ozma, the first SETI radio search"],["1961","Frank Drake presents his equation"],["1977","The Wow! signal is detected"],["1996","Robin Hanson proposes the Great Filter"],["2015","Breakthrough Listen begins"]],
s2:[["Rare Earth","In their 2000 book Rare Earth, Peter Ward and Donald Brownlee argued that simple life may be common but complex life is rare, because it needs a long list of fortunate conditions, such as a stable star, a large moon, plate tectonics and protection from comet impacts by Jupiter."],["Should we call out?","Some scientists argue that sending powerful messages to other stars, called METI, could be risky if alien civilizations were hostile, while others say any advanced civilization could already detect us. The debate has no clear answer."]],
sp:["The Wow! signal","On 15 August 1977, the Big Ear radio telescope in Ohio detected a strong, narrow radio signal from the constellation Sagittarius. Astronomer Jerry Ehman circled it on the printout and wrote ‘Wow!’. It lasted 72 seconds and was never heard again."],
g:[["Paradox","A situation that seems contradictory"],["SETI","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist"],["Technosignature","Evidence of technology from another civilization"]],
f:["Fermi asked ‘Where is everybody?’ in 1950.","The Drake equation estimates alien civilizations.","The Wow! signal was never detected again."],
fun:"Even traveling at a small fraction of the speed of light, a civilization could in theory visit every star system in the Milky Way within a few million years, a short time compared with the galaxy’s age.",
tl:"Tinatanong ng Fermi paradox kung bakit wala tayong nakikitang tanda ng sibilisasyon ng mga alien. Bilyun-bilyon ang bituin at planeta sa Milky Way, at maraming posibleng paliwanag, mula sa pagiging bihira ng buhay hanggang sa sadyang pagtatago ng mga alien."});

ADDART({id:"terraforming",t:"Terraforming",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Terraforming",r:["mars","venus","climchange","scifi","marsrovers"],
p:[["Making new Earths","Terraforming means changing a planet to make it habitable for humans."],["Mars is the main target","Most ideas focus on warming and thickening Mars’s atmosphere."],["Science fiction for now","Terraforming would take centuries or longer, if it is possible at all."]],
l:"Terraforming, meaning ‘Earth-shaping’, is the hypothetical process of deliberately changing the atmosphere, temperature, surface and ecology of a planet or moon to make it habitable for humans and other Earth life. The word was coined by science fiction writer Jack Williamson in 1942, and the idea was explored scientifically by astronomer Carl Sagan, who suggested in 1961 that algae could alter the atmosphere of Venus. Mars is the most discussed target: it has water ice, a day almost as long as Earth’s and a thin carbon dioxide atmosphere. Proposals to warm Mars include releasing greenhouse gases, placing mirrors in orbit to reflect sunlight onto the poles or spreading engineered particles to trap heat, which could eventually allow liquid water and plants. However, a 2018 NASA-funded study found that Mars does not have enough accessible carbon dioxide to thicken its atmosphere to Earth-like pressure, and Mars lacks a global magnetic field to protect an atmosphere. Venus would need its crushing, scorching atmosphere removed. Many scientists consider terraforming far beyond current technology, and some raise ethical questions about altering other worlds.",
s:[["Warming Mars","Mars’s average temperature is about −60°C, and its air pressure is less than 1 percent of Earth’s. A 2024 study suggested that releasing billions of tiny engineered nanorods of iron or aluminum into the Martian atmosphere could warm the planet by more than 30°C, enough for liquid water in places, much more efficiently than greenhouse gases. It would still take decades or more."],["Obstacles","Even if Mars were warmed, its atmosphere would remain too thin to breathe without oxygen, and building up oxygen through plants or microbes could take thousands of years. Without a magnetic field, the solar wind slowly strips the atmosphere away. Low gravity, about 38 percent of Earth’s, may also affect human health."],["Venus and others","Venus is similar in size to Earth, but its carbon dioxide atmosphere is 90 times thicker and its surface hot enough to melt lead. Ideas such as giant sunshades are extremely speculative. Some suggest that floating cities high in Venus’s atmosphere, where temperature and pressure are Earth-like, might be easier than terraforming."],["Ethics","If Mars has native microbes, terraforming could destroy them. Planetary protection rules aim to avoid contaminating other worlds. Some argue that humans should first take care of Earth, while others see terraforming as a long-term way to ensure humanity’s survival."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Earth-shaping’"],["Word coined","Jack Williamson, 1942"],["Main target","Mars"],["Mars air pressure","Less than 1% of Earth’s"],["2018 study","Not enough CO₂ to thicken Mars’s air"]],
d:[["1942","Jack Williamson coins ‘terraforming’"],["1961","Carl Sagan proposes changing Venus with algae"],["1976","NASA studies the habitability of Mars"],["1992","Kim Stanley Robinson’s Red Mars published"],["2018","Study finds Mars lacks enough CO₂ to terraform easily"],["2024","Study proposes warming Mars with nanorods"]],
s2:[["Terraforming in fiction","Kim Stanley Robinson’s Mars trilogy (1992–1996) describes centuries of terraforming Mars and the political conflicts it causes. Terraforming also appears in films and games such as Total Recall and Terraforming Mars."],["Practice on Earth?","Some scientists see geoengineering proposals on Earth, such as blocking some sunlight to cool the planet, as a kind of small-scale terraforming, and warn that the side effects of changing a planet are hard to predict."]],
sp:["Paraterraforming","Rather than changing a whole planet, ‘paraterraforming’ would enclose smaller areas, such as craters or canyons, under domes or roofs with breathable air, creating habitable spaces more quickly."],
g:[["Terraforming","Changing a planet to be like Earth"],["Greenhouse gas","A gas that traps heat"],["Planetary protection","Rules to avoid contaminating other worlds"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"]],
f:["The word terraforming was coined in 1942.","Mars’s air pressure is less than 1% of Earth’s.","Terraforming would take centuries or more."],
fun:"Some terraforming ideas propose crashing icy comets into Mars to add water and gases to its atmosphere.",
tl:"Ang terraforming ay pagbabago sa isang planeta upang matirhan ito ng tao. Karamihan ng ideya ay nakatuon sa pagpapainit at pagpapakapal ng atmospera ng Mars, ngunit aabutin ito ng daan-daang taon o higit pa, kung posible man."});

ADDART({id:"spaceelevators",t:"Space Elevators",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛗",w:"Space elevator",r:["elevators","rocket","sat","space","scifi"],
p:[["A cable to space","A space elevator would be a cable reaching from Earth into orbit."],["Cheaper than rockets","Climbers could carry cargo into space using electricity."],["No material yet","No known material is strong enough to build one on Earth."]],
l:"A space elevator is a proposed structure that would connect Earth’s surface to space with an extremely long cable, or tether, along which vehicles called climbers could travel into orbit without rockets. The cable would be anchored near the equator and extend beyond geostationary orbit, about 36,000 kilometers up, to a counterweight farther out, perhaps 100,000 kilometers away; Earth’s rotation would keep the cable taut, like a stone whirled on a string. Russian scientist Konstantin Tsiolkovsky imagined a tower to space in 1895, and engineer Yuri Artsutanov proposed the tether concept in 1960. Arthur C. Clarke popularized it in his 1979 novel The Fountains of Paradise. A space elevator could greatly reduce the cost of reaching orbit, but the cable would need to be stronger and lighter than any existing material. Carbon nanotubes and graphene might theoretically be strong enough, but they cannot yet be made in long enough, flawless lengths. A space elevator on the Moon or Mars, with lower gravity, would be easier and might be built with existing materials.",
s:[["How it would work","Climbers would grip the cable and climb using electric motors, powered by lasers beamed from the ground or by solar panels. A payload released above geostationary orbit would be flung outward with enough speed to reach other planets. A trip to geostationary orbit might take about a week."],["The material problem","The cable would have to hold its own enormous weight. Steel would snap under its own weight long before reaching orbit. Calculations show it would need a strength-to-weight ratio far beyond steel or Kevlar. Carbon nanotubes are about 100 times stronger than steel by weight in tiny samples, but long cables are another matter."],["Dangers","A space elevator would face lightning, storms, corrosion from atomic oxygen, impacts from space debris and satellites, and possible sabotage. If the cable broke, parts could fall to Earth or be flung into space. Its anchor could be on a movable ocean platform to dodge debris."],["Plans and competitions","NASA held space elevator competitions for tethers and climbers from 2005 to 2009. The Japanese company Obayashi announced a concept to build a space elevator by 2050. The International Space Elevator Consortium studies the idea."]],
k:[["Main parts","Cable, anchor, counterweight, climbers"],["Height","Beyond 36,000 km (geostationary orbit)"],["Early idea","Tsiolkovsky, 1895"],["Tether concept","Yuri Artsutanov, 1960"],["Possible materials","Carbon nanotubes, graphene"]],
d:[["1895","Tsiolkovsky imagines a tower to space"],["1960","Artsutanov proposes a space tether"],["1975","Jerome Pearson publishes a detailed design"],["1979","Arthur C. Clarke’s The Fountains of Paradise"],["2005","NASA begins space elevator competitions"],["2012","Obayashi announces a 2050 concept"]],
s2:[["Inspired by the Eiffel Tower","Tsiolkovsky was inspired by the Eiffel Tower, completed in 1889, to imagine a much taller tower reaching geostationary orbit, with a ‘celestial castle’ at the top."],["Lunar elevator","Because the Moon’s gravity is only one-sixth of Earth’s and it has no atmosphere, a lunar space elevator could be built with strong existing materials such as Zylon or Kevlar, linking the surface to a point in space."]],
sp:["Clarke’s novel","In The Fountains of Paradise, Arthur C. Clarke imagined a space elevator rising from a mountain on an island based on Sri Lanka, where he lived. He said space elevators would be built ‘about 50 years after everyone stops laughing’."],
g:[["Tether","A long cable"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit that matches Earth’s rotation, about 36,000 km up"],["Counterweight","A mass that balances another"],["Carbon nanotube","A tiny, extremely strong tube of carbon atoms"]],
f:["A space elevator would reach beyond 36,000 km.","No material is yet strong enough to build one.","Arthur C. Clarke popularized the idea in 1979."],
fun:"A space elevator cable could be thinner than a sheet of paper but wider than a ribbon, a design meant to resist damage from small debris.",
tl:"Ang space elevator ay mungkahing kable na aabot mula sa Earth hanggang sa orbit. Maaari itong magdala ng karga sa kalawakan gamit ang kuryente, ngunit wala pang materyal na sapat ang tibay upang itayo ito."});

ADDART({id:"keplertelescope",t:"The Kepler Space Telescope",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Kepler space telescope",r:["exoplanet","keplerlaws","scope","jwst","astrobio"],
p:[["Planet hunter","Kepler searched for planets around other stars."],["Thousands of discoveries","It found more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets."],["Planets are common","Kepler showed that most stars have planets."]],
l:"The Kepler space telescope was a NASA observatory launched on 6 March 2009 to discover planets orbiting other stars, especially Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist. Named after the astronomer Johannes Kepler, it stared continuously at a single patch of sky in the constellations Cygnus and Lyra, monitoring the brightness of about 150,000 stars. When a planet passed in front of its star, called a transit, the star dimmed very slightly, and Kepler’s sensitive camera detected the dip. After two of its four reaction wheels, which pointed the telescope, failed by 2013, engineers devised a new mission called K2, using the pressure of sunlight to help balance the spacecraft. Kepler ran out of fuel and was retired on 30 October 2018. It discovered more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets, more than any other telescope at the time, and thousands more candidates. Its results showed that planets are extremely common, that small planets are more common than giant ones, and that billions of Earth-sized planets may orbit in habitable zones in the Milky Way.",
s:[["The transit method","Kepler measured stars’ brightness every 30 minutes. A planet the size of Earth passing in front of a Sun-like star dims it by only about 0.01 percent, like a flea crossing a car headlight. Repeated dips at regular intervals revealed a planet and its orbital period, and the size of the dip revealed its size."],["Famous discoveries","Kepler-22b, announced in 2011, was the first planet found in the habitable zone of a Sun-like star. Kepler-186f, in 2014, was the first Earth-sized planet in a habitable zone. Kepler-16b orbits two stars, like Tatooine in Star Wars. Kepler-452b was called Earth’s ‘older cousin’."],["K2","With only two reaction wheels, Kepler could not hold steady. Engineers pointed it along its orbit so that sunlight pressed evenly on it, and it observed different fields for about 80 days each. K2 found hundreds more planets and studied supernovae and comets."],["Legacy","NASA’s TESS, launched in 2018, continues the search across almost the whole sky, finding planets around nearby bright stars. The James Webb Space Telescope studies the atmospheres of some planets found by these missions."]],
k:[["Launched","6 March 2009"],["Retired","30 October 2018"],["Method","Transit"],["Confirmed planets","More than 2,600"],["Stars watched","About 150,000 (main mission)"]],
d:[["2009","Kepler launches"],["2011","Kepler-22b announced in a habitable zone"],["2013","Second reaction wheel fails"],["2014","K2 mission begins; Kepler-186f announced"],["2018","Kepler retired"]],
s2:[["Tabby’s Star","In 2015, citizen scientists studying Kepler data found a star, KIC 8462852, that dimmed irregularly by up to 22 percent. Speculation about alien ‘megastructures’ made headlines, but further studies suggested dust was the cause."],["Citizen science","Volunteers in the Planet Hunters project searched Kepler data by eye and found planets that computer programs had missed, including a planet orbiting in a four-star system."]],
sp:["William Borucki","NASA scientist William Borucki proposed the Kepler mission in the 1980s and had it rejected several times because many doubted that the technology could detect such tiny dips in brightness. He persisted for nearly 20 years before it was approved."],
g:[["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun"],["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Habitable zone","Where liquid water could exist on a planet"],["Reaction wheel","A spinning wheel used to point a spacecraft"]],
f:["Kepler found more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets.","Kepler showed that most stars have planets.","Kepler watched about 150,000 stars at once."],
fun:"Kepler’s detector could notice a dimming as small as that caused by a flea passing in front of a car headlight seen from far away.",
tl:"Naghanap ang Kepler space telescope ng mga planeta sa paligid ng ibang bituin. Nakatuklas ito ng mahigit 2,600 kumpirmadong exoplanet at ipinakita nitong karamihan ng bituin ay may mga planeta."});

ADDART({id:"vesta",t:"Vesta",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪨",w:"4 Vesta",r:["asteroidbelt","ceres","ionengines","meteors","impactcraters"],
p:[["A giant asteroid","Vesta is the second-largest object in the asteroid belt."],["A planet that never finished","It has a core, mantle and crust like a small planet."],["Pieces on Earth","Some meteorites that fall to Earth come from Vesta."]],
l:"Vesta is one of the largest objects in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, about 525 kilometers across, second in mass only to the dwarf planet Ceres. It was discovered on 29 March 1807 by German astronomer Heinrich Olbers and named after the Roman goddess of the hearth. Vesta is the brightest asteroid as seen from Earth and can occasionally be seen with the naked eye. Unlike most asteroids, which are rubble piles, Vesta is a protoplanet: early in the solar system’s history it melted and separated into an iron core, a rocky mantle and a basaltic crust, like the rocky planets, but it never grew into a full planet. Its southern hemisphere is dominated by two enormous overlapping impact basins, Rheasilvia and Veneneia; Rheasilvia is about 500 kilometers wide and has a central peak about 20 kilometers high, one of the tallest mountains in the solar system. Debris from these impacts is the source of the HED meteorites, about 5 percent of meteorites that fall on Earth. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft orbited Vesta in 2011–2012, mapping it in detail.",
s:[["Dawn at Vesta","Dawn arrived at Vesta in July 2011 and orbited for 14 months, mapping its surface, measuring its gravity and studying its minerals with its cameras and spectrometers, before using its ion engines to fly on to Ceres."],["Rheasilvia","The Rheasilvia impact, about 1 billion years ago, blasted out about 1 percent of Vesta’s volume. Its central peak rises about 20 to 25 kilometers, comparable to Olympus Mons on Mars. The impact also created huge troughs that circle Vesta’s equator."],["Vesta meteorites","HED meteorites (howardites, eucrites and diogenites) have compositions matching Vesta’s crust. They allow scientists to study pieces of a protoplanet in the laboratory, revealing how Vesta melted and formed layers within a few million years of the birth of the solar system."],["Asteroid or protoplanet?","Vesta is not round enough to be classified as a dwarf planet, partly because of its giant impact basins, but its internal layers make it unlike typical asteroids. Scientists often call it the last surviving protoplanet of its kind."]],
k:[["Diameter","About 525 km"],["Discovered","Heinrich Olbers, 29 March 1807"],["Type","Protoplanet"],["Giant basin","Rheasilvia, about 500 km wide"],["Visited by","Dawn (2011–2012)"]],
d:[["1807","Olbers discovers Vesta"],["1970","Link between Vesta and HED meteorites proposed"],["1997","Hubble reveals a huge crater at Vesta’s south pole"],["2011","Dawn enters orbit around Vesta"],["2012","Dawn departs for Ceres"]],
s2:[["A snowman of craters","Three craters lined up on Vesta, nicknamed the ‘Snowman’, became one of the most recognizable features in Dawn’s images."],["Seeing Vesta","When Vesta is at its brightest, it can reach about magnitude 5.2, just bright enough to see with the naked eye from a very dark site. It is the only asteroid regularly visible without a telescope."]],
sp:["Named after a goddess","Vesta was the Roman goddess of the home and hearth, whose sacred fire in Rome was tended by the Vestal Virgins. The asteroid was the fourth to be discovered, after Ceres, Pallas and Juno."],
g:[["Protoplanet","A body that began forming into a planet but did not finish"],["Basin","A very large, shallow crater"],["Meteorite","A space rock that reaches the ground"],["Basalt","A dark volcanic rock"]],
f:["Vesta is the second-largest object in the asteroid belt.","About 5% of meteorites come from Vesta.","Vesta can sometimes be seen with the naked eye."],
fun:"The central peak of Vesta’s Rheasilvia crater is about 20 kilometers high, more than twice as tall as Mount Everest.",
tl:"Ang Vesta ang ikalawang pinakamalaking bagay sa asteroid belt. Mayroon itong core, mantle at crust tulad ng maliit na planeta, at ilang meteorite na bumabagsak sa Earth ay nagmumula rito."});

ADDART({id:"tianwen",t:"China’s Tianwen Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇨🇳",w:"Tianwen-1",r:["mars","marsrovers","spaceagencies","chandrayaan","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["Questions to heaven","Tianwen means ‘questions to heaven’, after an ancient poem."],["Mars in one try","Tianwen-1 orbited, landed and drove on Mars on its first attempt."],["Asteroids and samples","Tianwen-2 reached a near-Earth asteroid in 2026."]],
l:"The Tianwen missions are China’s program of robotic exploration of the planets, run by the China National Space Administration. The name, meaning ‘questions to heaven’, comes from a poem by the ancient Chinese poet Qu Yuan. Tianwen-1, launched on 23 July 2020, was China’s first independent mission to Mars. It entered Mars orbit in February 2021 and, on 15 May 2021, landed the Zhurong rover on the plain of Utopia Planitia, making China the second country, after the United States, to operate a rover on Mars and the first to orbit, land and deploy a rover on its first Mars mission. Zhurong explored for about a year, finding evidence of past water, before going into hibernation in May 2022 and not waking. Tianwen-2, launched on 29 May 2025, reached the small near-Earth asteroid Kamoʻoalewa, a ‘quasi-moon’ of Earth, in mid-2026 and plans to collect samples and return them to Earth before traveling on to a comet. Tianwen-3, planned for launch around 2028, aims to return samples from Mars by about 2031.",
s:[["Tianwen-1","The Tianwen-1 orbiter has mapped Mars and relayed data from the rover. The landing used a heat shield, parachute and rocket-powered descent, the ‘seven minutes of terror’. A small deployable camera photographed the orbiter in space, and Zhurong took a selfie with its lander."],["Zhurong","Named after a Chinese god of fire, the six-wheeled, solar-powered Zhurong rover traveled about 1.9 kilometers. Its ground-penetrating radar found layered deposits beneath the surface, and studies of its data suggested an ancient ocean shoreline in the area. Dust on its solar panels likely prevented it from waking after the Martian winter."],["Tianwen-2","Kamoʻoalewa, discovered in 2016, may be a fragment of the Moon blasted off by an ancient impact. Tianwen-2 is surveying it before attempting to collect samples, which are to be returned to Earth by capsule. The spacecraft will then use Earth’s gravity to head for the main-belt comet 311P/PANSTARRS, arriving in the 2030s."],["Tianwen-3 and beyond","Tianwen-3 plans two launches to land on Mars, collect samples, launch them into Mars orbit and return them to Earth, a mission NASA had also planned but scaled back because of costs. A future Tianwen-4 mission is planned to explore Jupiter’s system."]],
k:[["Name meaning","‘Questions to heaven’"],["Tianwen-1 launch","23 July 2020"],["Zhurong landing","15 May 2021"],["Tianwen-2 target","Asteroid Kamoʻoalewa"],["Mars sample return","Tianwen-3, about 2031"]],
d:[["2020","Tianwen-1 launches"],["2021","Zhurong lands on Mars"],["2022","Zhurong enters hibernation"],["2025","Tianwen-2 launches"],["2026","Tianwen-2 reaches Kamoʻoalewa"],["c. 2028","Tianwen-3 planned launch"]],
s2:[["China’s space program","China has become a major space power, building the Tiangong space station, landing the Chang’e probes on the Moon, including the first landing on the far side in 2019, and returning lunar samples. It plans crewed lunar landings before 2030."],["A quasi-moon","Kamoʻoalewa circles the Sun in an orbit so similar to Earth’s that it appears to loop around our planet as seen from Earth, though it is not truly a moon. It is only about 40 to 100 meters across and spins once every 28 minutes."]],
sp:["Qu Yuan’s questions","The ancient poem Tianwen, attributed to Qu Yuan, who lived about 2,300 years ago, consists of more than 170 questions about the heavens, nature, myth and history, reflecting curiosity about the universe."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that drives on another world"],["Quasi-moon","An asteroid that appears to orbit Earth"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Hibernation","A dormant, inactive state"]],
f:["Tianwen means ‘questions to heaven’.","Zhurong landed on Mars in 2021.","Tianwen-2 reached a quasi-moon of Earth in 2026."],
fun:"Zhurong’s selfie with its lander was taken by a small remote camera the rover dropped on the ground before driving away.",
tl:"Ang Tianwen ay programa ng China sa paggalugad sa mga planeta, at nangangahulugang ‘mga tanong sa langit’. Sa unang subok, nag-orbit, lumapag at nagmaneho sa Mars ang Tianwen-1, at narating ng Tianwen-2 ang isang asteroid noong 2026."});
